      "id": "wl-paras",
      "asset_name": "Paras Defence",
      "asset_type": "Stock",
      "sector": "Defence",
      "buy_price": 850,
      "current_price": 627.2,
      "quantity": 0,
      "purchase_date": "2026-03-20",
      "notes": "[Emerging] Retail favorite, volatile",
      "ticker": "PARAS.NS",
      "status": "watchlist",
      "buy_range": "800-900",
      "research": "**Executive Summary**\n\nParas Defence & Space Technologies is an ambitious Indian defence-engineering firm focusing on optics/optronics and defence electronics (including EMP protection and heavy engineering). Revenue climbed from ₹143.3 Cr (FY2021) to roughly ₹364.7 Cr in FY2025. Order backlog: ~₹928 Cr (FY25) vs ₹630 Cr (FY24). Trades at forward P/E ~80–90× — implying near-miraculous execution. Strong tailwinds from India's ₹7.85 lakh Cr defence budget (FY26-27, 75% domestic sourcing). However, razor-thin moats and sky-high valuation make it speculative. Verdict: Neutral/Avoid — not a buy unless execution is flawless and valuations compress.\n\n**Company Overview**\n\nTier-2 IDDM (indigenously designed, developed, manufactured) engineering group. Two main verticals: (1) Optics & Optronic Systems — night-vision lenses, IR imaging, space antennas, submarine periscopes (sole Indian maker), hyperspectral cameras. (2) Defence Engineering — rugged electronics, EMP-shielded shelters, missile fuzes, UAVs, anti-drone systems.\n- Key customers: DRDO, ISRO, L&T, BEL, HAL, Tata.\n- Reportedly sole Indian (or Asia-Pacific) supplier for many niche products.\n- ~600 workforce; CNC machining, optical coating labs, advanced assembly.\n- Subsidiaries: Paras Aerospace, Paras Anti-Drone, Quantico Tech (Israeli JV for inertial systems), Opel Tech (SG), Paras Avionics (2025).\n\n**Management & Ownership**\n\n- Listed: BSE 543367; Promoter stake ~58.9% (Mar 2024).\n- Chairman: Sharad Virji Shah (founder, non-executive). MD: Munjal Shah (son of Chairman). CFO: Sharad Shah's son-in-law — tightly-held family structure.\n- CRISIL credit rating: A-/Stable (LT), A2+ (ST).\n- Corporate actions: 1:2 share split (Jun 2025); QIP ₹135 Cr at ₹1,045/share (Oct 2024).\n\n**Key Orders & Partnerships**\n\n- L&T Electro-Optic Sights (Oct 2024): ₹305 Cr for 244 \"Sight-25HD\" naval gun sights.\n- DRDO Air-Defence Optics (Mar 2026): ₹80.28 Cr to develop optical system for air defence.\n- IRDE Border/UAV Systems: ₹53 Cr for surveillance & UAV payload systems.\n- Korea MoU (Mar 2026): Co-develop advanced optical systems with Green Optics Co.\n- IUAC MoU (Nov 2025): Develop MRI magnet & medical imaging systems.\n- Elbit Systems (Israel): Export contract for electro-optic systems.\n\n**Competitive Position & Moat**\n\nParas's moat is its IDDM niche: submarine periscopes, hyperspectral cameras, EMP shelters — products few or no other Indian companies make. But moat is narrow: BEL, HAL, or foreign suppliers could match quality at lower cost. Revenue (₹364 Cr) tiny vs BEL (₹20,268 Cr). No significant export footprint. Domestic dominance is policy-protected but vulnerable to shifts.\n- Key peers: BEL (₹3.11L Cr mcap), HAL (₹2.53L Cr mcap), BDL, IdeaForge (₹1,831 Cr).\n- BEL/HAL trade at low single-digit P/E vs Paras's ~80–90×.\n\n**Recent Financials**\n\nFY2021: Revenue ₹143.3 Cr, EBITDA margin 25.1%, PAT ₹15.8 Cr, ROE ~8.3%\nFY2022: Revenue ₹182.6 Cr, EBITDA margin 25.3%, PAT ₹27.0 Cr, ROE ~9.2%\nFY2023: Revenue ₹222.4 Cr, EBITDA margin 24.1%, PAT ₹35.9 Cr, ROE ~9.1%\nFY2024: Revenue ₹253.5 Cr, EBITDA margin 20.1%, PAT ₹30.0 Cr, ROE ~8.0%\nFY2025*: Revenue ~₹364.7 Cr, PAT ~₹61.5 Cr (+105% YoY), ROE ~11.7%\n5-year Revenue CAGR: ~27%. EBITDA margin declining trend (26% → 20%). PBT doubled in FY25 to ~₹83.6 Cr.\n\n**Valuation Metrics**\n\n- Market Cap: ~₹5,054 Cr; Forward P/E: ~80–90×.\n- Debt/Equity: ~0.03× (near debt-free post-QIP). Current Ratio: >2.0×.\n- ROCE (FY24): ~9.8%. EPS (FY24, post-split): ~₹6.9.\n- Order Backlog: ₹393 Cr (FY23) → ₹630 Cr (FY24) → ~₹928 Cr (FY25).\n- Priced for perfection — any slowdown = severe multiple compression.\n\n**Risks**\n\n- Execution risk: Defence projects face delays/overruns; Paras has limited bench strength.\n- Valuation risk: At 80–90× P/E, zero margin of safety. Any earnings miss = stock crash.\n- Concentration risk: A few big orders (L&T, DRDO) drive the entire story.\n- Supply chain: Specialty glass, chips still imported; shortages can stall delivery.\n- Competition: BEL or new private entrants could undercut on price or scale.\n- Geopolitical: ITAR-type export controls could block international contracts.\n- Promoter-heavy structure (58.9%) with family CFO — governance concentration risk.\n\n**Financial Snapshot**\n\n- Latest Price (Mar 2026): ~₹627 on NSE (market cap ~₹5,054 Cr)\n- FY25 Revenue: ~₹364.7 Cr (+44% YoY); PAT ~₹61.5 Cr (+105% YoY).\n- Order Book: ~₹928 Cr (FY25); 5-yr Revenue CAGR ~27%.\n- Promoter Stake: 58.9%; Debt/Equity: ~0.03×.\n- Verdict: Neutral/Avoid. Buy only if P/E normalises below 50× or growth remains flawless."
    },
    {
      "id": "wl-aequs",
      "asset_name": "Aequs",
      "asset_type": "Stock",
      "sector": "Defence",
      "buy_price": 110,
      "current_price": 123.06,
      "quantity": 0,
      "purchase_date": "2026-03-20",
      "notes": "[Growth] Tier-1 aerospace supplier (Airbus, Boeing); Q3FY26 +51% YoY rev, +353% EBITDA; Boeing KC-46 contract; BUY TP ₹145 by JM Financial",
      "ticker": "AEQUS.NS",
      "status": "watchlist",
      "buy_range": "100-120",
      "research": "**Executive Summary**\n\nAequs Ltd is a Bengaluru-based precision manufacturing company, primarily serving global aerospace OEMs (e.g. Airbus, Boeing, Safran, Collins, Honeywell) as a Tier-1 supplier. It also has a smaller consumer/industrial business (~11% of FY25 revenues). The stock trades around ₹120–140 in Mar 2026 (market cap ~₹9,000–9,500 Cr). Recent quarters show strong growth (Q3 FY26 revenues +51% YoY to ₹3262 m, EBITDA +353% to ₹381 m), driven by aerospace ramps. Order book stands at ~$814 m, including a new Boeing contract for KC-46 tanker doors. JM Financial initiates coverage with a Buy and ₹145 TP (~40× FY28 EBITDA). Key risks include slower ramp-up in consumer orders and customer concentration. Recommended buy range is ₹100–120.\n\n**Company Overview**\n\nAequs (formerly QuEST Global India) was founded in 2006 and is now one of India's largest end-to-end aerospace manufacturers. It operates integrated SEZ-based facilities (Belgaum, Hubli) offering forging, machining, surface treatment and assembly (\"AEQUS Aerospace Ecosystem\") for precision aerostructures. The company also makes consumer durables (cookware, electronics components) via joint ventures. As of FY25, ~89% of consolidated revenue came from Aerospace programs and ~11% from Consumer businesses. Aequs has long-tenured contracts with top OEMs (Airbus, Boeing, Safran, Collins, etc.) and recently partnered with Accel India/Vagus Defence to build UAVs for Indian defense.\n\n**Market Data**\n\nAs of Mar 20, 2026, Aequs shares traded around ₹120–140, having a 52-week range of about ₹113–169. The market cap is roughly ₹9,000–9,500 Cr. Daily liquidity is moderate (e.g. ~600k shares, ₹8–9 Cr on recent sessions). Promoters hold ~59.1%, FIIs ~18.5%, DIIs ~6–10%. The CEO & promoter is Aravind M. (25+ years aerospace), and MD is Rajeev Kaul; management has deep aerospace experience.\n\n**Recent Financials**\n\nFY24–25: Consolidated revenue ~₹9,450 m, a slight decline from FY24 (₹~9,650 m). FY25 saw a consolidated net loss of ₹1,023 m vs ₹108 m in FY24 (in part due to one-time IPO and labor costs). EBITDA margin remained low (~4–5%).\n\nQ3/FY26 (Dec 2025): Revenue ₹3,262 m (+51% YoY), EBITDA ₹381 m (+353% YoY) (12% margin vs 4%). 9M FY26 revenue ₹8,633 m (+28%), EBITDA ₹1,222 m (+85%). Improved execution and operating leverage drove margin expansion. Exports remain ~90% of sales, reflecting reliance on global OEMs. Losses narrowed (PAT –₹426 m vs –₹593 m 9M prior), with Q3 including one-time costs (labor law changes, IPO) of ₹167 m.\n\n**Key Orders & Partnerships**\n\n- Boeing 767-2C Doors: Aequs won a contract to make aft access doors for Boeing's 767-2C (KC-46A tanker) at WINGS India 2026. This underscores its Tier-1 defence credential.\n- Orderbook: Aerospace order book is cited at USD 814 m (₹~6,800 m), a healthy backlog with major OEMs.\n- UAV JV: Partnered with Accel India and Vagus Defence to design/manufacture UAVs for Indian defense use. This adds a new segment in defense electronics.\n\n**Clients & Partnerships**\n\nAequs lists a marquee client list: Airbus, Boeing, Collins Aerospace, Safran, Saab, Honeywell, etc. It has built long-standing relationships (15+ years with top 3 customers). It is also expanding the consumer portfolio (e.g. JV with Tramontina for cookware). The company emphasizes vertically integrated capability (forging + machining + assembly), and has global facilities (India SEZ + France, USA sites).\n\n**Competitive Position & Moat**\n\nAequs's moat is its integrated SEZ ecosystem that achieves high in-country value-add for aerospace; this was recognized by Airbus in 2016. Few Indian peers match its scale in aerospace. The Belagavi SEZ is India's first precision manufacturing zone. The long-term OEM tie-ups (validated by this Boeing win) provide entry barriers. However, the consumer segment is highly competitive and smaller-scale. Geopolitical or supply-chain shifts could impact aerospace flows, but strong customer anchors mitigate some risk.\n\n**Management & Ownership**\n\nPromoters (~59%) include founder-chairman Aravind Melligeri and management. Melligeri (CEO) has 25+ years in aerospace, key in building Belagavi SEZ. MD Rajeev Kaul has 22 years' experience in finance/aerospace. Board includes aerospace/engineering veterans (ex-Airbus, BMW, IAS officer). Institutional holding is moderate (FII ~18.5%, mutual funds ~5%). No single institutional block dominates, and promoter commitment is high given operational control.\n\n**Valuation Metrics**\n\nCurrent TTM P/E is extremely high (~188×) due to recent losses; forward P/E is not meaningful until profitable. EV/EBITDA based on JM Financial's framework is ~40× on FY28E, reflecting steep re-rate expectations. By comparison, established peers trade at ~20–30× EV/EBITDA (e.g. Amber at ~25×) and higher ROIC (see JM report). The stock's PEG is not applicable (negative EPS). The valuation assumes strong margin recovery (JMFS forecasts FY30 EBITDA margin ~33%).\n\n**Analyst Coverage & Targets**\n\nJM Financial initiated coverage (Mar'26) with a Buy and target ₹145. They argue the risk-reward is favorable post-IPO (75% value in aerospace segment) and see upsides from consumer ramp. No other recent broker reports were found, but Yahoo Finance shows no consensus EPS data (loss-making). Yahoo Estimates page (if any) is sparse.\n\n**Risks**\n\n- Slower consumer ramp-up (the big swing factor).\n- Aerospace demand slowdowns or order deferrals (e.g. aircraft production cuts) would impact revenues.\n- Customer concentration (Boeing/Airbus program dependencies) is a risk if a program is cut.\n- Rising raw material or forex costs could pressure margins (JMFS notes USD/INR moves).\n- Supply-chain disruptions (e.g. semiconductor shortages) may also impact operations.\n- The company is also leveraged (DE ~1.1), so interest rate hikes could burden.\n\n**Financial Snapshot**\n\n- Latest Price (Mar 2026): ~₹120–130 on NSE (market cap ~₹9,500 Cr)\n- FY25 Revenues: ₹9,411.9 m (down ~2.5% YoY); EBITDA margin ~4%.\n- Q3 FY26: Revenue ₹3,262 m (+51% YoY), EBITDA ₹381 m.\n- Order Book: ~$814 m (aerospace).\n- Valuation (TTM): P/E ~188, P/B 5.6; JMFS TP ₹145 (+18%).\n- Buy Range: ₹100–120 (targeting JMFS TP with margin of safety)."
    },
